exec the loader under the program's own name

The kernel records the path it was given to execve(2) as AT_EXECFN,
and a multi-call binary looks itself up by it.  proot executes the
loader in place of the guest program, so under this app's exec model -
PROOT_LOADER lives in the native library directory because the platform
refuses execve(2) on app data - every AT_EXECFN in the guest names the
loader.  Ubuntu 26.04's default coreutils provider is uutils, whose
dispatch reads AT_EXECFN, so its applets died with

    coreutils: unknown program 'libproot-loader'

while the applets still served by GNU binaries under the same rootfs
kept working: /usr/bin/rm -> gnurm was fine, /usr/bin/ls -> the uutils
multi-call was not.  "and the session runs as 'coreutils: unknown
program libproot-loader' instead of 'root'" is this, in whoami.

The loader already rewrites AT_EXECFN on the stack it hands over
(loader/loader.c), which is why getauxval(AT_EXECFN) and
/proc/self/comm are already right inside the guest.  The kernel's own
copy - what /proc/<pid>/auxv serves, and what a Rust program reads
through rustix's linux_execfn() - is written at execve(2) time and
cannot be changed from userspace afterwards, by the loader or by
anyone else.  So the name has to be right when the loader is exec'd.

Evidence, x86_64 Linux, the pinned fork + this patch set, the pinned
amd64 26.04 rootfs:

    $ PROOT_LOADER=.../libproot-loader.so proot -r rootfs -0 -b /proc /usr/bin/ls /
    coreutils: unknown program 'libproot-loader'
    $ PROOT_LOADER=.../libproot-loader.so proot -r rootfs -0 -b /proc /usr/bin/auxv-probe
    argv0=/usr/bin/auxv-probe
    getauxval(AT_EXECFN)=/usr/bin/auxv-probe          <- the stack copy, rewritten
    proc/self/auxv AT_EXECFN=.../libproot-loader.so   <- the kernel's copy
    comm=auxv-probe

    $ ln -s .../libproot-loader.so alias/ls
    $ PROOT_LOADER=.../alias/ls proot -r rootfs -0 -b /proc /usr/bin/ls /     # lists /
    $ PROOT_LOADER=.../alias/ls proot -r rootfs -0 -b /proc /usr/bin/cat /etc/hostname
    /etc/hostname          # cat acted as ls, though its argv[0] is cat:
                           # the applet comes from AT_EXECFN, not argv[0]

Hence the loader is now reached through a symbolic link named after
the program being executed, in a private temporary directory.  A link
rather than a copy, for two reasons: AT_EXECFN is the path as *given*
to execve(2) and not the resolved one, so a link keeps the program's
name while the kernel still executes the loader it points to; and the
loader must stay the single file the app installed, at a path the
platform allows to be execve'd.  The link is reused for every run of a
program in a session, and its directory is removed with the process'
other temporary files.

When no link can be made - an unwritable temporary directory, a name
longer than NAME_MAX, "." or ".." - the loader is exec'd under its own
path exactly as before.  Nothing else is ever exec'd.

Not upstreamed: no write access to oonid/pr; applied by
fetchLinuxSource in linux/build.gradle.kts (stacks on 0004).

What this does not fix, and should not be read as fixing: the *path*
is not restored, only the name.  A program that reads its own AT_EXECFN
for its name (multi-call dispatch, rustix's linux_execfn) is fixed; one
that reads it for its directory sees .../exec-<pid>-XXXXXX/ where a
native kernel would report the path the guest passed.  proot's own
AT_EXECFN rewrite, /proc/self/exe and /proc/<pid>/comm are unchanged,
and the guest's own argv[] was never involved.
--- a/src/proot/src/execve/enter.c
+++ b/src/proot/src/execve/enter.c
@@ -588,6 +588,96 @@
 }

 /**
+ * Return the path the loader has to be executed through for the sake
+ * of @tracee, or @loader_path itself when no better path can be
+ * built.
+ *
+ * The kernel records the path it is given to execve(2) as AT_EXECFN,
+ * and a program that multiplexes on its own name -- the uutils
+ * coreutils, busybox, ... -- looks itself up by it.  Since the loader
+ * is executed in place of the guest program, such a program would
+ * otherwise see the loader's name and refuse to run:
+ *
+ *     $ ls /
+ *     coreutils: unknown program 'libproot-loader'
+ *
+ * Hence the loader is reached through a symbolic link named after the
+ * program it stands in for.  A link rather than a copy, for two
+ * reasons: AT_EXECFN is the path as *given* to execve(2), not the
+ * resolved one, so a link keeps the program's name while the kernel
+ * still executes the loader it points to; and the loader stays the
+ * single file the app installed at a path the platform allows to be
+ * execve'd (app data does not, the native library directory does).
+ *
+ * The links live in a private temporary directory, removed along with
+ * this process' other temporary files.
+ */
+static const char *get_loader_exec_path(Tracee *tracee, const char *loader_path)
+{
+	static const char *alias_directory = NULL;
+	const char *guest_path;
+	const char *name;
+	char alias_path[PATH_MAX];
+	char link_target[PATH_MAX];
+	ssize_t status;
+
+	/* The name the guest asked for: the program itself, or the
+	 * script when a shebang line was expanded.  This is the same
+	 * path the loader reports to the program as AT_EXECFN.  */
+	guest_path = (tracee->load_info != NULL
+		? tracee->load_info->raw_path
+		: NULL);
+	if (guest_path == NULL)
+		return loader_path;
+
+	name = strrchr(guest_path, '/');
+	name = (name == NULL ? guest_path : name + 1);
+
+	/* Nothing to name a link after: keep the loader's own path,
+	 * as before this function existed.  */
+	if (name[0] == '\0'
+	    || strcmp(name, ".") == 0
+	    || strcmp(name, "..") == 0
+	    || strlen(name) > NAME_MAX)
+		return loader_path;
+
+	if (alias_directory == NULL) {
+		alias_directory = create_temp_directory(NULL, "exec");
+		if (alias_directory == NULL)
+			return loader_path;
+	}
+
+	status = snprintf(alias_path, sizeof(alias_path), "%s/%s", alias_directory, name);
+	if (status <= 0 || (size_t) status >= sizeof(alias_path))
+		return loader_path;
+
+	/* An up-to-date link is the common case: every run of a
+	 * program in a session goes through the same one.  */
+	status = readlink(alias_path, link_target, sizeof(link_target) - 1);
+	if (status < 0 || (size_t) status >= sizeof(link_target) - 1) {
+		/* Absent, or not a link at all: replace it.  */
+		unlink(alias_path);
+	}
+	else {
+		link_target[status] = '\0';
+		if (strcmp(link_target, loader_path) == 0)
+			return talloc_strdup(tracee->ctx, alias_path);
+
+		/* Left behind by another loader: replace it.  */
+		unlink(alias_path);
+	}
+
+	/* Never execute anything but the loader, whatever is in the
+	 * way: an unwritable temporary directory, or a name already
+	 * taken, leaves this process with the path it had before
+	 * instead of an unvetted one.  */
+	if (symlink(loader_path, alias_path) < 0)
+		return loader_path;
+
+	return talloc_strdup(tracee->ctx, alias_path);
+}
+
+/**
  * Extract all the information that will be required by
  * translate_load_*().  This function returns -errno if an error
  * occured, otherwise 0.
@@ -713,6 +803,14 @@
 	if (loader_path == NULL)
 		return -ENOENT;

+	/* ... but through a name the program can recognize as its
+	 * own, otherwise a multi-call binary reads the loader's name
+	 * from AT_EXECFN and refuses to run.  See
+	 * get_loader_exec_path().  */
+	loader_path = get_loader_exec_path(tracee, loader_path);
+	if (loader_path == NULL)
+		return -ENOMEM;
+
 	status = set_sysarg_path(tracee, loader_path, SYSARG_1);
 	if (status < 0)
 		return status;
